(A program reads its command line and its environment through the 's
 property syntax. Run it as: ./args_and_env -v -o out.bin main.vox)

(A flag is declared once, and Parse flags takes every declared flag off
 the command line, so arguments's all holds only what is left: here, the
 files to compile. A boolean flag takes no value; any other flag takes
 the word after it, and when a flag is given twice the last one wins. A
 lone -- ends the flags, so every word after it is positional.)
A flag called optimize is "-O" or "--optimize", it is a boolean with default false.
A flag called output is "-o" or "--output", it is a text with default "a.out".
A flag called jobs is "-j" or "--jobs", it is a number with default 1.
A flag called verbose is "-v" or "--verbose", it is a boolean with default false.
A flag called debug is "-g" or "--debug", it is a boolean with default false.
Parse flags.

A list called 'source files' is arguments's all.

If 'source files' is empty then,
    Print "Error: No source files provided.",
    Exit with 1.

If the verbose then,
    Print "Compiling with optimization: {the optimize}",
    Print "Output: {the output}",
    Print "Parallel jobs: {the jobs}".

For each 'source file' in the 'source files',
    Print "Compiling: {source file}".

Print "=== Arguments Properties Demo ===".

(The count includes the program's own name, which arguments's name reads)
A number called 'argument count' is arguments's count.
Print "Number of arguments: ".
Print the 'argument count'.

A text called program is arguments's name.
Print "Program name: ".
Print the program.

If arguments's count is greater than 1 then,
    a text called 'first argument' is arguments's first,
    Print "First user argument: {first argument}".

Print "".
If arguments's empty then,
    Print "No user arguments provided.".
Otherwise,
    Print "User arguments were provided.".

A text called 'last argument' is arguments's last.
Print "Last argument: ".
Print the 'last argument'.

Print "".
Print "=== Environment Properties Demo ===".

(An environment variable is read by its name)
A text called home is environment's "HOME".
Print "Home directory: ".
Print the home.

A text called user is environment's "USER".
Print "Current user: ".
Print the user.

A text called shell is environment's "SHELL".
Print "Shell: ".
Print the shell.

If the environment variable "DEBUG" exists then,
    Print "Debug mode is enabled via environment.".
Otherwise,
    Print "DEBUG environment variable not set.".

Print "".
A number called 'variable count' is environment's count.
Print "Total environment variables: ".
Print the 'variable count'.

A text called 'first variable' is environment's first.
Print "First env var: ".
Print the 'first variable'.

Print "".
Print "=== Practical Example ===".

(A name given on the command line wins over GREET_NAME in the
 environment, and either one wins over the world)
A text called 'greeting name' is "World".
If arguments's count is greater than 1 then,
    the 'greeting name' is arguments's first.
But if the environment variable "GREET_NAME" exists then,
    the 'greeting name' is environment's "GREET_NAME".

Print "Hello, ".
Print the 'greeting name'.
Print "!".
